{
  "image_name": "3eae413e2da1f1194832bd6a1aeb81e4.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes several visible pathways by which particles or drug carriers can cross an epithelial barrier from the lumen into underlying tissue. It compares five transport routes and highlights the key particle properties that influence each one.\n\nOn the left, receptor-mediated transcytosis is shown as ligand-decorated particles binding specific receptors on epithelial cells, being taken up into vesicles, and released on the tissue side. Paracellular diffusion is depicted as movement between adjacent cells, with polymer coatings and particle size appearing to control whether passage through the tight intercellular space is possible. Transcellular diffusion instead sends material directly through the cell membrane and cytoplasm, and this route is linked to lipophilicity. Adsorption-mediated transcytosis is shown as uptake driven by particle charge, again using vesicular transport across the cell. Finally, M cell antigen sampling illustrates specialized epithelial M cells transporting antigens to immune cells beneath the epithelium, including a macrophage.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that epithelial transport is not a single mechanism: different physicochemical features such as ligands, size, coatings, lipophilicity, charge, and antigen recognition determine how efficiently materials cross the barrier.",
